On Jul 22, 12:11 am, vintermann <vinterm...@gmail.com> wrote:
> The main reason you should use SVN over CVS is that SVN has atomic
> commits, so you won't wreck things because two people accidentally
> commited at the same time. Also, I hear branch management is much
> easier in SVN.

A project I worked on a couple years back adopted SVN and it worked
very well. It's very similar to cvs from a basic user perspective. In
fact, you can type alias cvs svn and it will work as you expect much
of the time, as the command syntax is often identical.

The only serious problem with SVN these days is that it doesn't have
the stable product integration that CVS does.  It would be nice if
there were a standard svn client plug-in for Eclipse, but at the
moment there are two that have roughly equal mindshare, and neither
one is really stellar.

Other systems like Bitkeeper are unnecessarily heavyweight for
projects involving only a few developers and a few tens of thousands
or couple hundred thousand lines of code. I really prefer to keep my
thinking about revision control uncomplicated. Mostly I am interested
in checkins and diffs between previous versions and/or branches. It's
nice to have a tool that will do that graphically. I prefer Eclipse
because it's also a good development environment, but there are many
other alternatives.



